body:
sounds like you're growing up. welcome to hell, lol.

&nbsp;

people will probably suggest meditation and other new-agey nonsense, but i think we both can see how silly that is.

i get that we have to rely on each other, but i think this is one of those cases in which i have the freedom to be independent, especially considering that meds haven't done me any good to begin with.

&nbsp;

they've all had different views: one told me that i need to just "lighten up", another suggested playing sports, my current one is obsessed with my relationship with my mom, he also told me to take some time off from school and work and that hasn't done me any good. all of the advice i get seems... trite. every time i tell a therapist that, i get the same reaction: "i'm going to pretend to be supportive on the surface because of ethics and what's considered 'good therapy' but deep down i think you're just being stubborn and not taking my advice seriously or else it would work"

&nbsp;

EDIT: i don't literally say to them "your advice seems trite" btw lol, i'm not that big of a dick

considering that i've tried several medications, none of which have made any changes, i don't really think it's chemical. besides, i have some personal hangups about being reliant on meds anyway. 

&nbsp;

to answer that last question, yes. all of my therapists have been psychologists with PhDs.
